        body: "Show buildings are engineered around the show. The structural engineer designs long-span roofs over theaters and dark rides, but the real coordination load is the show equipment: lighting grids, speaker clusters, scenic steel, and projection systems that hang from the structure and get heavier with every design revision. The engineer builds load allowances into the design and requires the show designers to stay within them — or come back for a recheck. Crowd-induced vibration is a serviceability design item for theater balconies, queue structures, and pedestrian bridges, where rhythmic crowd movement can excite the structure uncomfortably even when it is perfectly safe. The engineer tunes the structural frequency away from the crowd's step frequency rather than just adding steel.\n\nThemed facades get full structural engineering even though guests never see the steel. Artificial rockwork — shotcrete sculpted over a steel armature — and themed building fronts are designed for the full hurricane wind pressures the code assigns, with the armature engineered as a space frame and the shotcrete skin designed to stay attached in design winds. The engineer details the connection between the themed skin and the building behind it so the two move together without the skin peeling off, and specifies corrosion protection for the armature in Florida's humid, salt-laden air. Every themed element is also checked as potential windborne debris: the design must ensure that if the code's wind provisions are met, the facade stays where it was built. Like everything else in the park, the themed structure is permitted, inspected, and observed — the magic is in the show design, but the safety is in the engineering.",
