        heading: "Panels, Connections, and the Load Path",
        body: "The complete hurricane load path in a tilt-wall building runs from the windward panel face, through the panel, into the foundation at the base and the roof diaphragm at the top, then through the diaphragm to the shear-resisting panels, and down to the foundations. The engineer designs every link in that chain for the code's wind loads, and sizes each connection — panel to footing, panel to roof, panel to panel — so the failure mode is ductile yielding of the steel rather than brittle pullout from the concrete. Embed plates are the critical detail: the engineer sizes the plate, the studs or anchors behind it, and the concrete breakout capacity, because a connection that is strong in the steel but weak in the concrete fails at the concrete.\n\nPanel-to-panel joints do structural and waterproofing work simultaneously. Structurally, the connections between adjacent panels transfer in-plane shear so the wall line acts as a unit; the engineer details chord steel or welded connections across the joints for that purpose. Against water, the joints get backer rod and sealant designed for the joint movement the panels will see in thermal cycling and wind flexure — a joint that is structurally sound but leaks in every tropical storm is a failed design from the owner's perspective. The engineer coordinates the structural joint width with the sealant manufacturer's movement capability, because a sealant asked to stretch beyond its rating will fail no matter how well it was installed.",
