        heading: "Edge Metal, Parapets, and the Load Path Below",
        body: "The roof edge deserves its reputation as the failure initiation point. The engineer designs the edge metal system — the fascia, gravel stop, and cleats — for the wind pressures at the roof perimeter, specifying the gauge, the fastener type and spacing, and the tested assembly rating. Coping on parapets gets the same treatment: it is a wind-loaded component, and the engineer details its attachment so the coping cannot lift off and expose the wall below. Where the design includes a parapet, the engineer also checks the parapet itself as a cantilevered element for out-of-plane wind, including the coping weight and the flashing details that keep water out of the wall.\n\nBelow the membrane, the structural load path must be continuous and verified. The engineer checks the deck attachment — the screws or welds holding the metal deck, or the fasteners in a wood deck — against the uplift the assembly delivers, because the best membrane system in the world is useless if the deck leaves the joists. Then each connection down the chain: joist to beam, beam to column or wall, wall to foundation. In existing buildings this often means a structural evaluation of the original construction, since the engineer cannot assume a 40-year-old roof deck was fastened to today's standards. The permit submittal in the high-velocity hurricane zone includes the Florida product approvals for every assembly component, and the engineer cross-references the approval numbers to the pressure zones on the drawings — the plan reviewer checks exactly this, and mismatched approvals are one of the most common causes of roofing permit rejections.",
