        body: "The space under an elevated Keys home is the most regulated square footage in the house. The floodplain rules allow it to be used only for parking, storage, and building access — never living space — and the engineer designs the enclosure accordingly. Breakaway walls must be designed to fail under flood loading at a force below what would damage the foundation: the engineer calculates the wall's failure capacity and demonstrates it is less than the lateral capacity of the pile system, so the wall sacrifices itself before the flood can push the house over. This is genuinely engineered failure — the wall is designed to break at the right load, which means the connections are detailed to release rather than to hold.\n\nFlood vents are the companion detail: openings that let floodwater flow into and out of the enclosed area so pressure equalizes instead of building against the walls. The engineer sizes the vent area to the code's requirement per square foot of enclosed space and positions vents to actually flow — vents blocked by grade or landscaping do not count, and the engineer notes the maintenance obligation on the drawings. Above the design flood elevation, the engineer keeps the discipline going: all electrical panels, HVAC equipment, and ductwork sit above the flood level, and flood-resistant materials are specified for everything below it. The elevation certificate — prepared from a licensed survey and reviewed against the engineer's design elevation — is the document the floodplain administrator checks first, and in Monroe County and the Keys municipalities that review is rigorous: the local ordinances add freeboard and restrictions beyond the national minimums, and the engineer designs to the local standard from day one.",
