        heading: "Caps, Drainage, and the Permit Stack",
        body: "The cap is the most visible part of the wall and a structural element in its own right. The engineer designs the reinforced concrete cap as a continuous beam spanning between the wall panels or piles, tying the system together so individual panels cannot displace independently. It is designed for the loads it will actually see: wave impact on its face, uplift and lateral force from overtopping flow, pedestrian or vehicular loads on its top, and the salt spray environment that attacks its reinforcement — so the concrete mix, cover, and reinforcement detailing all reflect marine exposure. The cap-to-wall connection is detailed to develop the full design forces, because a cap that separates from the wall in a storm leaves the panels unbraced at the top.\n\nDrainage behind the wall is the detail that prevents the most common post-storm failure. When surge recedes faster than the groundwater behind the wall can drain, the trapped water pushes the wall waterward with hydrostatic pressure the wall was not designed to resist alone — the engineer has seen walls survive the storm's arrival only to fail on its departure. Weep holes through the wall, or a drainage aggregate zone with filter fabric behind it, give that water a path out; the filter prevents the drainage path from becoming a soil-loss path, which would recreate the piping failures that plague older walls. The permit stack for Gulf-front work is the full set: City of Naples or Collier County building permits for the structure, state environmental authorization below the high-water line, and federal review where navigable waters are involved. The engineer runs these tracks in parallel from the start, since the environmental reviews are typically the schedule drivers, and each authority having jurisdiction approves on its own criteria.",
