        body: "Soil pressure is the permanent load. The retained earth behind the wall pushes laterally with a force set by the soil type, the wall height, and the surcharge from whatever sits on top, whether a warehouse, a parking lot full of trucks, or stacked containers. The geotechnical investigation provides the soil parameters, and the engineer designs for the worst credible combination: full surcharge, high groundwater after heavy rain, and the channel dredged to its maximum depth in front of the wall. Seismic loads are negligible in Miami, so the design effort concentrates on getting the soil-structure interaction right.\n\nWater loads cycle constantly. Tides raise and lower the river level, and a rapid drawdown, when the river drops faster than groundwater behind the wall can drain, leaves the full hydrostatic pressure acting with no balancing water in front. Boat wakes add dynamic impact, and berthing vessels push fenders and bollards that transfer horizontal loads into the cap and wall. Storm surge is the extreme event: the wall and its anchors are checked for the surge water level, and the engineer confirms the wall will not be overtopped and outflanked at its ends, because water going around the wall erodes the retained soil from behind.",
