        body: "Steel sheet-pile is the default for the Miami River because it installs fast from the water or the bank, handles the depth, and pairs with tieback anchors that keep the wall section economical. The engineer selects the pile section for bending moment and deflection, sets the tip elevation from the geotechnical profile with enough embedment for stability and scour, and designs the wale and tieback system to carry the anchor loads into competent soil or rock. Tiebacks are proof-tested during installation, because an anchor's capacity in Miami's variable limestone and sand cannot be assumed from a catalog. The concrete cap is reinforced to distribute berthing and bollard loads and to protect the pile tops.\n\nThe corrosion zone gets its own design effort. The splash zone just above mean water corrodes steel fastest, so piles receive high-performance coatings through that band, a sacrificial steel thickness is added to the design section, and impressed-current or sacrificial cathodic protection is commonly specified for the submerged zone. Concrete walls and caps use durable mixes with high cover over reinforcement. The design life, often 50 years for commercial bulkheads, is stated explicitly, and the owner gets an inspection and maintenance plan, because even the best corrosion system needs monitoring to reach its design life in brackish water.",
