        body: "Coquina is a young sedimentary rock of cemented shell fragments, soft enough to cut with a saw when quarried and hardening with exposure, which is why the Spanish chose it for the Castillo de San Marcos and the city's colonial buildings. Tabby served where stone was scarce: burned oyster shell made the lime, mixed with sand, water, and more shell for aggregate, poured in lifts into wooden forms. Both materials breathe, absorbing and releasing moisture with the seasons, and both depend on lime mortar joints that are weaker and more permeable than the units they join. Understanding this assembly as a moisture-managed system, not as defective modern masonry, is the foundation of every preservation decision.\n\nRepointing is where preservation succeeds or fails. The engineer specifies a lime mortar, often a natural hydraulic lime or lime-putty blend, with sand graded and colored to match the historic joints, and proportions softer than the masonry units. Deteriorated joints are raked out by hand or with small tools, never with aggressive grinders that widen joints and scar the stone, and the new mortar is packed in lifts and tooled to the historic profile. The specification requires test panels reviewed by the preservation architect before production repointing begins, because mortar that looks right on paper can read wrong on the wall, and repointing is meant to last decades.",
