        heading: "Water Comes First: Stormwater and the High Water Table",
        body: "Stormwater engineering near the Everglades is about mimicking nature's timing. Before development, rain soaked into the ground and moved slowly as sheet flow; after development, roofs and pavement shed it instantly. The district's criteria force the design to erase that difference: the site must detain the design storms and release water at pre-development rates, and the discharge must meet water-quality standards, particularly for phosphorus, which damages the Everglades at strikingly low concentrations. The engineer designs a treatment train, swales and forebays that settle solids, detention ponds sized for volume control, and littoral plantings that take up nutrients, with calculations proving each stage's performance.\n\nThe high water table complicates everything below grade. Ponds must be designed with the seasonal high water table as the starting condition, not the water level on the day of the survey, or they will have no storage when the wet season arrives. Underground stormwater chambers need buoyancy checks so they do not float. Utility trenches become French drains unless they are sealed and bedded properly, carrying water where the design never intended. The civil engineer coordinates the grading plan so the finished site drains to the stormwater system and not to the neighbor's lot or the adjacent wetland, because misdirected drainage is both an engineering failure and a permit violation.",
